ps":0,"is_error":false,"num_turns":27,"subtype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"`work/supervisor.py`, `work/NOTES.md` and `work/predictions.json` are written. On all 4,451 recorded states the supervisor picks the same phase as the network, so I expect test TTS to be about the same as running the network under the shield alone.\n\n- **What it does:** it keeps the network's top-scoring phase whenever the rules allow it. When the rules leave only one phase, it passes the network's choice through, because the shield will force that one phase anyway.\n- **When it overrides:**\n  - If the network's choice is illegal and several phases are legal (max green reached, or several phases overdue), it takes the legal phase the network scores highest.\n  - If a phase will be forced right after the network's pick anyway, it serves that phase first only when its queue is clearly bigger (1.5× margin, queue per second of green).\n  - On the recorded states neither case ever fired.\n- **Checks:**\n  - Replayed every recorded state: outputs are always 0–3, and the applied phase never differs from the shield-only run.\n  - Edge cases (min green, max green, one or two overdue phases, the queue swap firing and not firing, list inputs) all pass.\n  - The replay is open-loop only, since no simulator could be run.\n- **Main finding:** the network switches at its first chance. Most of the loss comes from the 15 s pedestrian minimum stretching through greens from 12 s to 17 s, which no supervisor can avoid. Max green never binds. Forced service of starved phases mostly comes in the order that costs least anyway.\n- **Predictions (veh-h):** low 35.9, nominal 47.1, high 57.9, which are the dev shield-only values. Expect ±1–2% from new seeds.","ttft_ms":1162,"type":"result","duration_ms":701458,"uuid":"20545f2a-a19c-4a67-8738-efec2661b706","ttft_stream_ms":1013,"time_to_request_ms":93,"time_to_request_phases_ms":{"other":3,"system_prompt":9,"resume_other":0,"process_user_input":2,"inp